From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hjviewer <haejin.cho@yonsei.ac.kr>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 19:43:25 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 38/47] hjviewer: minimum version: a PC refuses controllers
 below their platform's minimum

A PC that holds the version policy refuses, at login, a controller whose app is below
what the policy blocks for the controller's platform. hbbs refuses old controllers
before that, but only this holds a controller that came by IP address, and a newer app
that could not get the policy and so did not hold itself back.

- hjviewer_update/refuse.rs decides, by the family rule of the trust rules: a controller
  is refused below LIMIT(now), the lowest of what the entries of the platform the login
  names block at that time, and is told the lowest min of them. That platform is
  Windows, Android (both Android entries together, the APK's and Google Play's, for the
  PC cannot tell the two apart) or iOS; other platforms are not looked at. Before a
  deadline only a controller below the version the grace period starts at (grace_min)
  is refused; where the two Android entries cross (the APK's higher minimum still in its
  grace period, Play's lower one at once), only what both block is refused until the
  APK's deadline. The version counts as hbb_common::get_version_number counts it, but
  without that function's overflow on what a stranger sends: a version that cannot be
  counted is 0, the oldest of all, as hbbs has it. Without a policy, or without an entry
  for the platform, nobody is refused. A refusal also has the policy checked, a check
  nobody waits for and so under the usual limits whoever knocks (one request a minute at
  most, none for half an hour after a failure): a minimum set too high by mistake and
  taken back by a root declaration no longer keeps a PC with nobody at it refusing
  everyone for up to 6.6 hours, until its next six-hourly check. The login itself is
  decided by the policy the process holds; the next one finds the corrected policy (the
  trigger is in table 3-1 of the minimum version design).
- hjviewer_update/texts.rs writes the answer, the login error an old app shows as it
  comes: which version the PC takes, where the platform's update is, and the mark that
  keeps a session from connecting again by itself. It takes the minimum from the policy
  and shows the controller's own version only if that is digits and dots.
- connection.rs asks right after the ID whitelist, in the login and where switched
  sides come back: before the permissions, the connection window, the password and a
  login by account key.
- client.rs: a refusal for the version, by hbbs or by a PC, is shown as the app's own
  text and makes the app check the policy at once (on_refusal, on_login_refusal).
- rendezvous_mediator.rs: a process that takes connections checks the policy by itself,
  half a minute after its start and then every six hours.
- lang/ko.rs: the two results of --update in Korean.

A build without the update keys does none of this: its logins and its errors are those
of 1.5.1.

Tests: the rule, the texts and the counting of versions with the rule given by hand:
the empty version before the deadline, a version below grace_min, a policy without a
deadline, which drops grace_min with it, and the Android cases with Play's deadline
later, Play ahead of the APK, and the two crossing. A test of the hook's way through the
process's policy in a build that takes part (compiled with HJV_SERVER and the update
keys, as the pinned test build of the checks is): the process is given a policy signed
with the tests' key, and refuse_controller refuses by it once its deadline has passed by
the device's clock, in a PC's words, and lets in a controller at the minimum, one of a
platform that is not checked, and everyone while the deadline is still ahead. It also
holds that only a refused login asks for a check: a controller let in while the
process's own check is due leaves no check asked for and the due one where it was, and a
refused one has the check asked for and put off by that minute. In any other build the
test has nothing to ask and ends at once. The hook lines themselves (connection.rs,
client.rs, rendezvous_mediator.rs) are reached by the E2E alone.

(Minimum version design, work package C2; trust rules, work package TR.)

